  8. WHAT THIS REPORT EXERCISES: THE ONE LIVE ENGINE UNIT TEST
  This is the module's ONE and ONLY unit test that bootstraps a real workflow engine, and it declares
  exactly one test method. Its editorial job is therefore to be precise about how little it proves.
  The context configuration on line 59 of the source loads the test context named in section 1. That
  context does not stub the engine and does not mock it. It builds a data source over an IN-MEMORY
  DATABASE, hands that data source and a matching transaction manager to a genuine Spring backed process
  engine configuration with schema auto update switched ON and the background job executor switched OFF,
  produces a real process engine from a factory bean, and exposes the repository, runtime, task, history
  and management services that the class injects. The fixture method then DEPLOYS a process definition
  taken from the module's own main resources, and that definition file is verified present in the module's
  main resources activiti directory. The single test method starts a process instance from it, completes
  two human tasks in sequence, and asserts on task name, assignee, due date, identity link count, identity
  link type and candidate group, then asserts that exactly one historic process instance exists and that
  no runtime instance remains.
  CONSEQUENCE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT: that outcome is entirely environment dependent and CANNOT be
  inferred from source. Whether the engine starts, whether it creates its schema, whether the deployment
  parses, whether the process reaches its end state, and how long any of it takes are facts only a run
  produces. This report therefore records WHICH test method existed at the baseline, and NOT what it did.
  No pass may be implied from it, and none is.
  The workflow engine's version does NOT change in this migration. A version bump without a demonstrated
  incompatibility is out of scope just as firmly as an unjustified omission, and none was demonstrated for
  this engine, so the root aggregator's engine version property is identical at the base commit and at the
  migrated revision. Because the engine version is constant AND this module's source and POM are untouched,
  any difference between this report and its migrated counterpart points at the RUNTIME itself, or at a
  root level library change that reaches this class. The candidate that reaches this class is the Spring
  test runner on line 58, since 11 of the module's 16 unit test classes load a Spring context and the
  framework advances by patch level inside its existing generation rather than changing generation.
  This class does NOT use the module's mocking library. It is one of exactly two exceptions among the
  sixteen unit test classes, the other being the task rules test, so no mocking library exposure may be
