  10. HONEST LIMITS OF THIS EVIDENCE, AND THE NUMERIC HONESTY STATEMENT
  This is the statement this file exists to make, and it is stated plainly rather than glossed.
  The module's live engine unit coverage is EXACTLY ONE TEST, and it is this one. Meanwhile the module's
  most engine proximate unit test, the workflow engine data access test, is DORMANT at the baseline under a
  pre-existing class level ignore annotation, so it contributes no engine exercise at all. And the FIVE
  integration classes that exercise the engine END TO END produce NO REPORT in this folder whatsoever:
  CandidateGroupIT, TaskActivitiIT, TaskDiagramIT, BuckslipActivitiIT and BuckslipArkcaseIT, re-derived
  with
      find acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-task-plugin/src/test/java -name '*IT.java'
  A unit test run stops at the test phase, while the integration test runner's goals bind later: the root
  aggregator declares that runner with no explicit phase, so both of its goals take their default phases,
  and both of those fall after the test phase. An absent integration test is neither a pass nor a failure
  and may NEVER justify a test exclusion.
  THEREFORE: the unit test evidence in this folder does NOT clear the workflow engine residual risk. That
  risk is assigned to the DEPLOYMENT and SMOKE gates, and this archive must preserve that distinction
  rather than blur it. One test method against an in-memory database, whose outcome was never even
  observed, is not end to end engine verification, and presenting it as more would be a false clearance of
  the one risk the migration explicitly left unverified.
  What the record actually says about that risk: the workflow engine is the OLDEST load bearing engine in
  the reactor, drives the 36 live process definitions counted in section 9, and could not be bootstrapped
  for testing without a database, so it is classified as residual UNVERIFIED risk rather than declared
  safe. Three findings BOUND that concern without eliminating it: the process definition corpus contains
  zero script tasks and zero script format declarations, so the engine never requests a scripting engine
  and the platform's removal of its bundled script engine cannot reach it; the engine's persistence layer
  reflects over the application's own domain classes through ordinary unrestricted reflection; and it reads
  no class bytes and touches no platform internal package. Bounding a risk is not clearing it, and this
  file claims only the former.

  11. TWO FALSE POSITIVES A REVIEWER SHOULD NOT DRAW
  First, this reactor contains NO test retry tooling. A recursive search of all 145 POMs for a rerun count
  setting returns zero, and no retry plugin is declared anywhere, so a failure observed at the baseline is
  real and reproducible, and failing fast is safe. The reactor does carry a Spring retry APPLICATION
  library, declared as a root version property and consumed by exactly three child modules, none of them
  this one, and it has nothing whatever to do with rerunning tests. In a task and workflow module, where
  retry semantics are part of the domain vocabulary and the engine itself has a job executor, that
  inference is especially tempting and especially wrong; note that the test context described in section 8
  switches that job executor OFF, so nothing in this class retries anything either.
  Second, coverage exclusions are NOT test exclusions. The root coverage plugin configuration excludes
  eight package globs from coverage MEASUREMENT only, and disables no test; none of the eight names this
  module. Bindingly related: the coverage agent hands its configuration to the test runner through the
  default argument line property export, so the pinned unit test runner declaration must NOT set a literal
  argument line element. Setting one would silently sever instrumentation and leave the coverage check
  goal evaluating an empty data set against its minimum ratio, which reports a pass while measuring
  nothing. Instrumentation must be verifiably live, and a recursive search of all 145 POMs for a literal
  argument line element returns zero.

